10.2.5.m:
The first [layer has] one hundred and eighty-six; the second, seventy plus ten;

the third should have thirteen and one hundred, the wise say;

the fourth is said to be one hundred and one and three bricks;

and the highest layer has three hundred and fifty-six [bricks].

10.3.4.m:
The prick between the middle and the two loops / is called the Gāyatra measure.

On half a puruṣa with an aratni, / the square is measured with it.

By the enlargement of the ends of the wings and tail, / with the Gāyatra [measure] and other arrows;

10.3.7.m:
Of the chariot-wheel fire altar, / which was concisely stated by Viṣṇu:

Now of the creator placed [within], / the other three times outward, outward;

in whose circle are dissolved / seven and a half men by the wise;

With half a man one should draw the hub; / then it is within the range of the layer.

The rim is beyond the spokes / by a sixty-third, the axle-socket;

by one hundred and thirty, accompanied by twenty, / and by four and a half additional [units];

or one should measure with aṅgulas the middle; one should make the drawing with twenty.
